        "answer": 2,
        "passage": "People today live longer than at any time in history. In the early twentieth century, the average life expectancy in many countries was barely fifty years, but it now exceeds seventy-five in most parts of the world. Better medicine, cleaner water and improved nutrition have all contributed to this <b>remarkable</b> change. However, living longer is not the same as living well, and researchers are now asking what makes the extra years worthwhile.\n\nOne answer lies in lifestyle. Studies of communities where many people reach the age of ninety show that they rarely follow strict diets or train in gyms. Instead, they stay active through everyday tasks such as gardening and walking, eat mostly plants and share meals with family and friends. <u>Their long lives seem to owe less to special treatments than to ordinary habits repeated over many years.</u> Strong social ties also appear to protect them from loneliness, which is itself a risk to health.\n\nSleep and stress are equally important. [I] Adults who regularly sleep for seven to eight hours tend to have healthier hearts and stronger memories than those who sleep much less. [II] Long-term stress, on the other hand, keeps the body in a constant state of alert and can weaken the immune system. [III] Deep breathing, short walks and time spent with friends are all effective ways of reducing its effects. [IV] Doctors therefore recommend treating rest and relaxation as seriously as diet and exercise.\n\nOf course, no habit can guarantee a long life. Genes, income and the quality of local healthcare all matter, and <b>they</b> are largely beyond an individual's control. Even so, research suggests that a large share of age-related illness can be delayed by the choices people make. The most encouraging message is perhaps that it is rarely too late to begin: people who improve their habits in middle age still gain additional healthy years."
