        "passage": "There is an old saying that laughter is the best medicine, and modern science is beginning to agree. When we laugh, the body releases chemicals that reduce pain and improve mood. A good laugh also relaxes the muscles and increases the amount of oxygen that reaches the brain, so that many people feel calmer and more alert afterwards.\n\nResearchers have studied the effects of laughter in hospitals as well. Patients who watch comedy programmes often report less pain than <b>those</b> who do not, and some doctors now encourage visits from clowns and storytellers. <u>Although laughter cannot cure a serious disease, it can make treatment easier to bear.</u> Some hospitals have even created special \"laughter rooms\" where patients can relax with funny films.\n\nLaughter also strengthens relationships. Sharing a joke with friends or family builds a sense of closeness that is difficult to find in other ways. In the workplace, teams that laugh together tend to cooperate more easily and to solve problems more creatively. For this reason, some companies now organise games and <b>informal</b> gatherings to improve the mood of their employees.\n\nNot everyone, however, finds the same things funny, and humour that hurts others can damage trust rather than build it. Experts therefore advise people to choose their jokes with care. They also point out that laughter is not a <b>substitute</b> for other healthy habits, such as sleeping well, eating a balanced diet and taking regular exercise. Nevertheless, a few minutes of laughter every day is a simple and enjoyable way to look after our well-being."
