        "passage": "Around the world, populations are growing older. Thanks to better healthcare, people in many countries can now expect to live well into their eighties, and the number of people over sixty-five is increasing faster than any other age group. This <b>trend</b> brings both opportunities and challenges for societies.\n\nOne of the main concerns is the cost of looking after so many older people. Pensions and hospital care require large sums of money, while fewer young workers are available to pay for them through taxes. <u>Unless governments plan carefully, the burden on the working population could become too heavy to bear.</u> Some countries have therefore raised the retirement age, though this decision is often unpopular.\n\nAt the same time, older people bring valuable strengths. [I] Many are healthy and energetic enough to continue working part-time or to volunteer in their communities. [II] Retired teachers, for example, help children with their reading at local libraries. [III] Grandparents also look after young children, which allows parents to go to work. [IV] These contributions are rarely counted in economic statistics, yet they are worth a great deal.\n\nTo make the most of this potential, societies must remove the barriers that keep older people out of work and public life. Employers who refuse to hire anyone over fifty are wasting valuable talent, and <b>they</b> may soon find themselves short of experienced staff. Flexible working hours, further training and age-friendly public spaces would help older citizens stay active, independent and respected."
