        "answer": 3,
        "passage": "In Japan, a growing number of people practise a simple form of relaxation known as forest bathing. It involves no swimming or special equipment: participants simply walk slowly through a wooded area, breathing deeply and paying attention to the sounds, smells and colours around them. The practice began in the 1980s and has since spread to many other countries.\n\nScientists who have studied forest bathing report encouraging results. Participants usually show lower blood pressure and lower levels of stress hormones than <b>those</b> who spend the same amount of time walking in a city. <u>It appears that natural surroundings help the body to calm down more effectively than urban ones.</u> Some researchers believe that chemicals released by trees may also strengthen the immune system, although more evidence is needed.\n\nForest bathing is also highly <b>accessible</b>. You do not need to travel to a remote mountain; a city park or a quiet riverside path can offer many of the same benefits. It is suitable for people of almost any age or level of fitness, and it costs nothing to try. For many busy urban residents, it is a practical way of fitting a little nature into daily life.\n\nNot everyone is convinced. Some doctors remain <b>sceptical</b>, pointing out that people who choose to walk in forests may already be healthier or less stressed than others. Supporters reply that even if the effects are modest, the practice is harmless, free and pleasant. The safest conclusion, perhaps, is that spending time among trees does no harm and may do a good deal of good."
