        "answer": 3,
        "passage": "Walk into any park in the early morning and you will see people checking their wrists. Fitness trackers, small devices that count steps, measure heart rate and record sleep, have become <b>widespread</b> in the past decade. Millions of users rely on them to set goals and follow their progress, and many health apps offer to turn a smartphone into a personal trainer.\n\nThe benefits are real. Seeing the number of steps rise during the day encourages many users to move more, and reminders to stand up help those who sit for long hours. <u>Some studies have found that people who use trackers tend to be more active than those who rely on their own judgement.</u> Sharing results with friends also adds a social element that keeps people motivated.\n\nHowever, the devices are not perfect. [I] Their sensors can be inaccurate, especially when measuring calories or sleep stages. [II] Users who pay too much attention to the numbers may also become anxious, checking their screens constantly and worrying when a target is missed. [III] For these people, a tracker designed to promote health may actually reduce peace of mind. [IV] Some doctors therefore suggest checking the device only once or twice a day.\n\nPrivacy is another concern. Trackers record details of their owners' daily routines and health, and the companies that make the devices may store or sell this data. Experts therefore advise users to read the privacy settings carefully. In the end, a tracker can only support healthy habits; it cannot replace the effort of the person wearing it. Used wisely, <b>they</b> can be useful tools, but they are not a cure-all."
