        "passage": "For thousands of years, people in Asia have used herbs, roots and leaves to treat illness. Ginger is used for stomach problems, green tea for tiredness, and many families keep dried plants at home for colds and fevers. Traditional medicine is still popular today, even though modern hospitals are available in every large town.\n\nIts supporters point out that many modern drugs were originally discovered in plants. Aspirin, for example, was developed from a substance found in willow bark. Herbal remedies that have been tested carefully are more trustworthy than <b>those</b> that have not. <u>A long history of use, however, does not prove that a remedy is safe or effective, and only scientific testing can show which ones really work.</u>\n\nSafety is the main worry. Some herbs can react badly with other medicines, and a dose that suits an adult may be <b>hazardous</b> to a child. Because natural products are often sold without strict controls, buyers cannot always be sure what a package contains. Doctors therefore advise patients to tell them about any herbal products they are taking.\n\nMany experts now support an <b>integrated</b> approach in which traditional and modern medicine work side by side. A patient with a long-term illness, for example, might take prescribed tablets while using gentle herbal teas or massage to ease the symptoms. In this way, the strengths of each tradition can be combined and the weaknesses of each can be reduced."
