        "passage": "Few discoveries have saved as many lives as vaccines. Before they were introduced, diseases such as smallpox and polio killed or disabled millions of people every year. Smallpox has since been completely <b>eradicated</b>, and polio cases have fallen by more than ninety-nine per cent.\n\nA vaccine works by training the body to recognise a germ without making the person ill. It contains a weakened or inactive form of the germ, and the immune system learns to fight it. <u>If the real germ appears later, the body can respond quickly, often before any symptoms develop.</u> Vaccination also protects people who cannot be vaccinated, such as newborn babies, because when most of a community is immune, the disease has little chance of spreading.\n\n[I] Despite these successes, some people are afraid of vaccines. [II] False stories spread quickly on social media and make parents hesitate to protect their children. [III] Doctors point out that serious side effects are extremely rare and that the risks of the diseases themselves are far greater. [IV] Some countries have therefore launched campaigns to explain how vaccines are tested and approved.\n\nExperts stress that vaccination works best when the whole community takes part. When enough people are vaccinated, <b>they</b> protect not only themselves but also those who are too young or too ill to receive a vaccine. Seen in this way, getting vaccinated is both a personal choice and a responsibility towards others."
