        "answer": 2,
        "passage": "Human eyes were designed for looking at distant objects in natural light, not for staring at bright screens a few centimetres away. Today, however, many teenagers spend up to seven hours a day in front of screens. Eye doctors report that more young people than ever before complain of tired, dry eyes and headaches, and rates of short-sightedness are rising fast.\n\nThe main cause appears to be the way we look at screens. When concentrating, people blink far less often than usual, so the surface of the eye dries out. Children who spend most of their free time indoors are also more likely to become short-sighted than <b>those</b> who play outside. <u>Natural daylight seems to protect young eyes in a way that artificial light cannot.</u>\n\nFortunately, the damage can be limited. Many doctors recommend the \"20-20-20 rule\": every twenty minutes, look at something about six metres away for twenty seconds. Adjusting the brightness of the screen, sitting at a <b>proper</b> distance and having regular eye tests are also helpful.\n\nParents and schools have a role too. Some schools now <b>restrict</b> the use of tablets in class and organise outdoor lessons, while parents can set screen-free times at home. Such measures do not mean rejecting technology. Rather, they aim to ensure that the benefits of digital devices do not come at the expense of young people's eyesight."
