        "answer": 1,
        "passage": "Most people know that sweets, cakes and soft drinks contain sugar. What many do not realise is how much sugar is hidden in foods that do not taste particularly sweet. Tomato sauce, breakfast cereals, flavoured yoghurt and even bread can contain <b>surprising</b> amounts, and these small quantities quickly add up over the course of a day.\n\nHealth experts say that adults should limit added sugar to a small amount each day, yet many people consume two or three times the recommended amount. <u>Eating this much sugar over a long period raises the risk of weight gain, tooth decay and type 2 diabetes.</u> Children are particularly at risk because their taste preferences are formed early, and a sweet-loving child often becomes a sweet-loving adult.\n\nFortunately, there are simple ways to cut down. [I] Reading food labels is a good start, since sugar often appears under other names, such as syrup or fructose. [II] Choosing water or unsweetened tea instead of soft drinks removes a large share of the sugar many people take in. [III] Fresh fruit can satisfy a craving for something sweet while providing fibre and vitamins. [IV] Cutting down gradually works better than stopping suddenly, because taste buds adjust within a few weeks.\n\nFood manufacturers also have a role to play. Several countries now require clear labels showing the sugar content of every product, and some have introduced taxes on sugary drinks, which has encouraged companies to adjust their recipes. Such measures may not change habits overnight, but <b>they</b> make it easier for shoppers to make informed choices."
