        "passage": "Ask any teenager how they feel at seven in the morning, and the answer is likely to be tired. Scientists explain that this is not simply laziness. During adolescence, the body's internal clock shifts, so that teenagers naturally fall asleep later at night and wake later in the morning. Yet most schools start early, forcing students to get up when their bodies are still <b>drowsy</b>.\n\nThe consequences are serious. Students who regularly sleep less than eight hours are more likely to fall asleep in class, to make mistakes and to feel unhappy. <u>Researchers have also linked a lack of sleep in adolescence with a higher risk of accidents and with problems of attention that can last for years.</u> Some experts compare the effect with that of a mild illness that never goes away.\n\nA number of schools have therefore experimented with later start times. [I] In one study, students who began lessons an hour later slept longer, reported better moods and achieved slightly higher marks. [II] Attendance also improved, and fewer students arrived late. [III] Not every community was happy with the change, however, since a later finish disturbs sports training and parents' working schedules. [IV] Even so, many schools have kept the new timetable.\n\nChanging the timetable is not the only answer. Families can help by limiting evening screen time and keeping consistent bedtimes, while students can avoid caffeine in the afternoon. Even so, many experts argue that a later start would give <b>those</b> who struggle most the biggest benefit."
