        "answer": 1,
        "passage": "For much of the twentieth century, mental health problems were rarely mentioned in public, discussed instead in hushed tones or not at all. In recent years, however, this silence has grown noticeably less <b>prevalent</b>, as public figures, schools and employers increasingly speak openly about anxiety, depression and stress.\n\nOne reason for this shift is a growing body of research linking untreated mental health problems to serious physical consequences, from disrupted sleep to weakened immune systems. <u>Recognising these physical effects has made it harder for workplaces and schools to dismiss mental health as a purely personal matter unrelated to performance.</u> As a result, many institutions now provide counselling services that would have seemed unusual only a decade ago.\n\n[I] Still, significant barriers to seeking help remain. [II] In many communities, admitting to a mental health struggle is still seen, unfairly, as a sign of personal failure. [III] Cost is another obstacle, since therapy and medication remain unaffordable for many families even where services exist. [IV] Rural areas, in particular, often lack any specialist at all within a reasonable travelling distance.\n\nDespite these challenges, campaigners remain cautiously optimistic. Schools that have introduced mental health education report that students become noticeably more willing to discuss their feelings and seek support early, before <b>minor</b> difficulties grow into more serious problems. Many experts now argue that treating mental and physical health as equally important, rather than ranking one above the other, offers the clearest path towards healthier, more resilient communities."
