        "answer": 1,
        "passage": "Sleep has long been treated as a minor detail of daily life, something to be sacrificed first whenever exam season or a demanding job schedule intrudes. Researchers studying teenagers, however, increasingly describe this attitude as a <b>mistake</b> with consequences far beyond simple tiredness the next morning.\n\nAccording to several long-term studies, teenagers who regularly sleep fewer than seven hours a night show measurably higher rates of anxiety and low mood than those who sleep longer. <u>Poor sleep appears not merely to accompany emotional difficulty but to actively worsen it, creating a cycle in which anxiety disturbs sleep and exhausted, disturbed sleep in turn deepens anxiety.</u> Breaking this cycle, researchers argue, is often a more effective first step than addressing either problem alone.\n\nPart of the difficulty lies in habits that feel <b>harmless</b> in the moment. Scrolling through a phone in bed, students often report, helps <b>them</b> relax before sleep; in reality, the bright screen light delays the body's natural release of the hormone that induces drowsiness. Late-night study sessions, similarly intended to help, frequently push bedtime back far enough to undo whatever extra revision was achieved.\n\nSchools are beginning to respond. Some have shifted start times later in the morning, a change that researchers link to measurable improvements in students' mood and concentration. Parents, meanwhile, are increasingly encouraged to treat a fixed bedtime as seriously as a fixed mealtime, rather than as a rule that can be abandoned whenever convenient. Small, consistent changes of this kind, experts suggest, do more for a teenager's mental health than any single dramatic intervention ever could."
