        "answer": 1,
        "passage": "Mornings in many households are rushed, and breakfast is often the first casualty of a tight schedule. Yet nutritionists increasingly describe skipping breakfast as a habit with consequences that are far from <b>trivial</b>, particularly for teenagers whose brains and bodies are still developing rapidly.\n\nSeveral studies comparing students who eat breakfast with those who do not have found consistent differences in classroom performance. <u>Students who begin the day with a proper meal tend to concentrate for longer periods and perform noticeably better on memory-related tasks than classmates who arrive at school hungry.</u> Researchers suggest that the brain, having fasted for roughly twelve hours overnight, simply requires fuel before it can function at its best.\n\n[I] Not all breakfasts, however, are equally beneficial. [II] Meals high in sugar, such as sweetened cereal or pastries, may provide a brief burst of energy followed by a noticeable slump within an hour or two. [III] Meals combining protein, whole grains and fruit tend to provide steadier, longer-lasting energy throughout the morning. [IV] Even a simple combination, such as eggs with wholemeal toast, can make a measurable difference to a student's concentration by mid-morning.\n\nSchools have begun to respond in small but meaningful ways. Some now offer a short breakfast period for students who arrive too late, or too rushed, to eat at home, recognising that a hungry student is rarely in the best position to learn. <b>This</b> small change, several schools report, has already improved attendance at morning classes. Parents, meanwhile, are encouraged to prepare whatever is feasible the night before, since even a piece of fruit and a handful of nuts is far better than nothing at all. The evidence, researchers argue, is clear enough: a few minutes spent on breakfast can shape how well a student performs for the rest of the morning."
