        "answer": 0,
        "passage": "Thirst is often the last signal the body sends, not the first, meaning that by the time a student feels thirsty, mild dehydration may already be affecting concentration. Researchers studying classroom performance have grown increasingly interested in this <b>overlooked</b> factor, finding that something as simple as water intake can measurably influence how well students think.\n\n<u>Even a small drop in hydration, equivalent to losing around two per cent of the body's normal water content, has been shown to reduce attention span and short-term memory in students.</u> Headaches and fatigue, both common complaints during long school days, are also frequently linked to insufficient fluid intake rather than tiredness alone.\n\nDespite this evidence, many students drink far less water during the school day than recommended. Lessons rarely include a planned pause for drinking water, and some students report feeling too <b>self-conscious</b> to ask permission to leave the classroom for a drink. Limited access to refill points in older school buildings compounds the problem further.\n\nSimple changes appear to help considerably. Schools that have installed accessible refill points and permitted water bottles on desks report fewer complaints of headaches and improved afternoon concentration, according to several small surveys. <b>This</b> simple step costs schools very little compared with its apparent benefits. Encouraging students to refill a bottle at every natural break in the day, rather than waiting until genuinely thirsty, is now a common piece of advice offered by school nurses across the country."
