        "answer": 2,
        "passage": "Doctors have coined a new term for an old problem made worse by modern habits: 'text neck.' The condition develops when a person spends long periods looking down at a phone or tablet, placing <b>considerable</b> strain on the neck and upper spine. What was once seen mainly in older adults with years of poor posture is now increasingly diagnosed in teenagers and even younger children.\n\nThe mechanics behind the problem are straightforward. The average head weighs around five kilograms when held upright, but tilting it forward to look at a screen can multiply the effective strain on the neck several times over. <u>The further the head tips forward, the greater the pressure placed on the muscles and joints that were never designed to support such weight for hours at a time.</u> Repeated daily over months or years, this strain can lead to chronic pain, reduced flexibility and, in severe cases, early spinal changes.\n\n[I] The good news is that text neck is largely preventable. [II] Simple adjustments, such as raising a phone closer to eye level rather than tilting the head down, can significantly reduce strain. [III] Taking regular breaks to stretch the neck and shoulders also helps release built-up tension. [IV] Physiotherapists increasingly recommend strengthening exercises for the muscles that support the upper back, since stronger muscles are better able to resist the pull of poor posture.\n\nBecause the underlying cause is behavioural rather than purely physical, lasting change requires more than a single stretch or exercise session. Schools that have introduced short posture breaks between lessons report that students return to class visibly more comfortable and, according to some teachers, more attentive as well. <b>This</b> simple habit, several teachers note, costs nothing but a few minutes of class time. Addressing text neck early, before habits become deeply ingrained, is likely to spare today's teenagers from chronic pain later in life."
