        "passage": "Eye strain has become one of the most common complaints among students who spend long hours reading from screens for schoolwork. Optometrists describe the discomfort as largely <b>preventable</b>, provided that a few simple habits are built into a student's daily routine.\n\n<u>The best-known of these habits, known as the 20-20-20 rule, advises looking away from a screen every twenty minutes to focus on something roughly twenty feet away for at least twenty seconds.</u> The logic behind the rule is simple: constantly focusing at a fixed, close distance tires the small muscles inside the eye, and shifting focus occasionally allows <b>them</b> a brief, genuine rest.\n\nBlinking, surprisingly, plays a role as well. Studies have found that people blink significantly less often while looking at a screen than while reading printed text, which can leave the eyes feeling dry and <b>irritated</b> by the end of a long study session. Consciously blinking more often, together with keeping screens at a comfortable distance and reducing glare from nearby lights, can further ease the strain.\n\nNone of these habits require any special equipment or expense, which is precisely why optometrists find them frustratingly underused. Reminder apps, sticky notes on a laptop, or simply setting a recurring timer can help turn the 20-20-20 rule into an automatic habit rather than something remembered only after the eyes have already begun to ache. Small, consistent adjustments of this kind, experts argue, do more for long-term eye comfort than any single dramatic change."
