        "answer": 2,
        "passage": "In many Asian families, parents have clear ideas about what their children should study. Medicine, engineering and business are often seen as safe choices that lead to stable careers. Teenagers, however, are growing up in a world of fast-changing technology, and many dream of becoming game designers, content creators or musicians. This difference of opinion is a common cause of <b>tension</b> at home.\n\nParents' concerns are understandable. They have seen jobs disappear and fear that creative careers are too uncertain to support a family. Many also feel responsible for guiding their children away from mistakes that they themselves made. <u>What looks like control to a teenager may actually be an expression of care.</u> Nevertheless, young people need room to discover their own strengths.\n\n[I] Experts suggest that families treat career choice as a shared project rather than a battle. [II] Teenagers can research the training and income of the jobs that interest them and present their findings calmly. [III] Parents, in turn, can ask questions out of curiosity rather than criticism. [IV] A visit to a workplace or a talk with someone in the profession can also help both sides to see the reality.\n\nCompromise is often possible. A teenager may agree to study a practical subject while developing a creative skill on the side, or parents may allow a trial period during which the young person tests the chosen path. Many families find that once the argument becomes a conversation, <b>they</b> discover that their goals are not so different: both want a secure and fulfilling future for the young person."
