        "answer": 1,
        "passage": "Every generation develops its own way of speaking. Words that sound normal to teenagers may leave their grandparents puzzled, and expressions used fifty years ago can seem old-fashioned today. Slang is not new: what is new is the <b>rapid</b> speed at which online communities create and spread it.\n\nLanguage experts say that slang serves an important purpose. It helps young people to feel part of a group and to separate themselves from adults, just as clothes and music do. <u>Rather than damaging a language, slang usually adds colour to it, and many words that once seemed shocking have entered ordinary dictionaries.</u> Parents who understand this are less likely to see their children's way of speaking as a sign of poor education.\n\nProblems arise only when slang prevents communication. A teenager who uses abbreviations in a job application may seem careless, and a grandparent who does not understand a message may feel excluded. Teachers therefore encourage students to learn when each style is appropriate, so that <b>they</b> can move comfortably between formal and informal language.\n\nTechnology also helps older people to keep up. Online dictionaries explain new expressions within minutes, and many grandparents enjoy learning the words their grandchildren use. In return, young people can learn old proverbs and sayings that are full of <b>wisdom</b>. A conversation in which each side teaches the other becomes a pleasure rather than a battle."
