        "passage": "Technology has changed family life in ways that earlier generations could hardly have imagined. Parents who grew up writing letters now find their children sending dozens of messages a day, and grandparents who once gathered around a single television now see family members watching different screens in the same room. These changes have created a <b>noticeable</b> gap in the way generations communicate.\n\nMany parents worry that children spend too much time online and neglect face-to-face conversation. Teenagers, on the other hand, argue that their devices keep them in touch with friends, help with homework and offer entertainment. <u>Both views contain some truth, which is why simply banning devices rarely solves the problem and often makes it worse.</u> A more effective approach is to agree on sensible rules together.\n\n[I] Families that manage the gap successfully usually share a few habits. [II] They set aside device-free times, such as meals or the hour before bed. [III] Parents show interest in their children's online activities instead of simply criticising them. [IV] Teenagers, in turn, are willing to explain how apps and games work.\n\nTechnology can also bring generations closer when it is used well. Video calls allow grandparents who live far away to watch their grandchildren grow up, and shared online photo albums let relatives follow family events. Teenagers who teach older relatives new skills usually gain patience and confidence, and <b>they</b> often receive warm appreciation in return."
