        "answer": 1,
        "passage": "Some schools and companies have started mentoring programmes in which older and younger people learn from each other. A retired engineer may advise a group of students on a science project, while a student may help an older person to use a laptop. Both sides discover that they have more in common than they expected.\n\nMentoring is not new, since apprentices have always learned from skilled masters. What is new is that the flow of knowledge goes in both directions. Younger people often excel at technology, while older people possess experience and judgement that come from years of work. Older people who take part in such programmes often report feeling more useful and more connected than <b>those</b> who stay at home. <u>Young participants say that talking with a mentor makes them less afraid of making mistakes because they see that everybody has faced difficulties.</u>\n\nHowever, successful mentoring depends on good organisation. Participants must be matched carefully, according to interests and personalities, and they need clear goals so that meetings do not become aimless chats. Meetings should take place regularly, and both sides should feel free to say honestly when something is not working. Without such preparation, even a friendly relationship may become <b>awkward</b>.\n\nThe benefits extend beyond the individuals. Companies that mentor young employees tend to keep skilled staff for longer, and communities where generations meet regularly are often more tolerant and cooperative. As populations age, such links will become more <b>vital</b>. By learning to listen to one another, people of all ages can turn the generation gap into a source of strength."
