        "passage": "As banking, healthcare appointments and family communication move rapidly onto mobile apps, many older adults feel left behind by technology. Researchers and educators studying intergenerational digital mentoring note that this trend has become increasingly <b>recognised</b> in recent years, as schools and youth clubs organise weekend sessions where teenagers teach grandparents practical smartphone skills.\n\nCloser observation of everyday situations reveals why intergenerational digital mentoring matters so much in practice. <u>When teenagers slow down and let older learners tap the screen themselves, frustration quickly turns into pride.</u> This pattern shows that practical engagement with intergenerational digital mentoring often reshapes long-held assumptions far more effectively than passive instruction.\n\n[I] When schools and communities introduce structured initiatives focused on intergenerational digital mentoring, participants often respond with unexpected enthusiasm. [II] Many report that working through realistic examples together helps clarify points that previously caused confusion or disagreement. [III] Coordinators observe that this collaborative atmosphere encourages even hesitant participants to contribute their own perspectives. [IV] Over time, these shared experiences build habits of cooperation that extend well beyond the original programme.\n\nSome sceptics occasionally question whether short community programmes on intergenerational digital mentoring can produce <b>meaningful</b> results without major structural reforms. Organisers generally reply that while large-scale policy matters, equipping everyday people with practical skills and mutual understanding remains the clearest foundation for sustainable progress."
