        "answer": 2,
        "passage": "Imagine a city where everything you need, such as shops, schools, clinics and parks, is no more than fifteen minutes away on foot or by bicycle. This is the idea behind the \"fifteen-minute city\", a concept that has attracted interest from planners around the world. Its supporters argue that it would cut traffic and give residents more free time. Early reports suggest that people in such neighbourhoods walk more and feel healthier than <b>those</b> who live in car-dependent suburbs.\n\nAt present, many people travel long distances for even simple tasks because homes, workplaces and shops are located in separate zones. This pattern was created in the twentieth century, when planners believed that cars would solve every transport problem. <u>Today, however, the cost of long commutes, in both time and pollution, is leading many cities to reconsider that assumption.</u> Several cities around the world have introduced plans inspired by the idea.\n\nCritics, however, raise some objections. Making every neighbourhood self-sufficient may be <b>impractical</b> in very large cities, where specialised hospitals, universities and workplaces cannot be duplicated everywhere. There is also a risk that popular neighbourhoods will become more expensive, forcing poorer families to move out. Supporters reply that the concept is a flexible guide rather than a rigid rule, and that it can be adapted to local conditions.\n\nWhatever its limits, the idea has changed the way people think about urban life. It reminds planners that a city is first of all a place for people, not for vehicles. Small changes, such as wider pavements, safer crossings and local markets, can make neighbourhoods more <b>vibrant</b> and bring neighbours together."
