        "passage": "As cities expand, farmland retreats, and food has to travel long distances to reach urban consumers. Urban farming offers a different approach: growing vegetables, herbs and even fish inside the city. Rooftop gardens, vertical farms in buildings and community plots in unused corners are all examples. Supporters believe that such farms could make cities more <b>self-sufficient</b>.\n\nThe advantages are considerable. Food grown close to consumers is fresh and needs little transport, which reduces fuel use and pollution. <u>Plants on rooftops and walls also keep buildings cooler and absorb rainwater, which lessens the burden on drains during storms.</u> Many urban farms recycle water and use less land than traditional agriculture, so they use resources efficiently.\n\n[I] Urban farming also has social benefits. [II] Community gardens bring neighbours together and give residents a chance to learn about growing food. [III] Children who take part in school gardens usually gain a better understanding of where their meals come from. [IV] Some projects employ elderly people or the unemployed, offering them useful work and a small income.\n\nYet urban farming faces challenges. Rents for city land are high, and starting a vertical farm requires expensive equipment and electricity. Some crops grow poorly without sunlight, and soil in cities may contain harmful substances that must be tested before planting. Because of these limits, most experts expect urban farms to supply a modest share of food rather than to replace traditional agriculture, and <b>they</b> advise cities to treat them as a useful addition."
