        "answer": 1,
        "passage": "Self-driving vehicles, which use sensors, cameras and software to navigate without a human driver, were once a topic for science fiction. Today, test vehicles operate on public roads in several countries, and some companies already run driverless taxis in limited areas. Supporters believe that the technology will transform urban transport within a few decades.\n\nThe most important promised benefit is safety. Most road accidents are caused by human error, such as tiredness, distraction or speeding. Computers do not get tired or distracted, so in theory, self-driving cars should cause fewer accidents than <b>those</b> driven by people. <u>Autonomous vehicles could also reduce congestion by communicating with each other and by choosing the most efficient routes.</u> Elderly people and those with disabilities might gain new independence.\n\nYet there are serious obstacles. Software must be able to deal with unexpected events, such as a child running into the road or heavy rain that confuses the sensors. Laws must decide who is responsible when an accident happens: the owner, the manufacturer or the programmer. Many drivers also remain <b>sceptical</b>, saying that they would not trust a machine with their lives.\n\nThere may also be social effects. Millions of people work as taxi, bus and lorry drivers, and their jobs could disappear. Cities might need fewer car parks, freeing space for parks and housing, but if driving becomes cheaper and easier, more vehicles may enter the roads. Governments therefore need to plan carefully so that the technology brings real <b>benefits</b> rather than new problems."
