        "passage": "The Association of Southeast Asian Nations, known as ASEAN, was established on 8 August 1967 in Bangkok, when five countries signed a declaration to work together for peace and development. Its founding members were Indonesia, Malaysia, the Philippines, Singapore and Thailand. In the following decades, other countries joined, and today the organisation is one of the most <b>influential</b> regional groups in the world.\n\nViet Nam became a member on 28 July 1995, a step that marked its opening to the region and the world. Since then, the country has taken part in ASEAN meetings, hosted major summits and joined cooperation programmes in trade, education and culture. <u>Joining the organisation has allowed Viet Nam to attract foreign investment, expand its exports and strengthen its voice in regional affairs.</u> Vietnamese students have also benefited from scholarships and exchange programmes offered by member states.\n\n[I] In 2015, ASEAN formally announced the ASEAN Community, which rests on three pillars: political-security, economic and socio-cultural cooperation. [II] The economic pillar aims to create a single regional market in which goods, services and skilled workers can move more freely. [III] The socio-cultural pillar focuses on education, health and the protection of cultural heritage. [IV] The political-security pillar seeks to maintain peace through dialogue rather than conflict.\n\nDespite these achievements, ASEAN faces challenges. Member states differ greatly in size, income and political systems, which can make agreement difficult. Disputes at sea and the effects of climate change also require careful cooperation. Even so, countries that cooperate closely generally achieve better results than <b>those</b> that act alone."
