        "passage": "At the end of 2015, ASEAN announced the establishment of the ASEAN Economic Community, one of the three pillars of the ASEAN Community. Its goal is to create a single market and production base in which goods, services, investment and skilled workers can move more <b>freely</b> between member states. For a region with hundreds of millions of people, such integration promises considerable economic benefits.\n\nThe most visible change has been the reduction of taxes on goods traded within the region. Most tariffs between member states have been removed, so a Vietnamese company that exports clothing to Thailand pays little or no tax. <u>Cheaper trade encourages companies to form regional supply chains, in which different stages of production take place in different countries.</u> A phone, for instance, may be designed in one country, assembled in another and packaged in a third.\n\n[I] The community has also made it easier for companies to invest in neighbouring countries. [II] Many Vietnamese businesses now operate factories, shops or restaurants in other ASEAN states. [III] At the same time, foreign investors have brought capital, technology and jobs to Viet Nam. [IV] Agreements on recognising professional qualifications are intended to make it easier for engineers, nurses and accountants to work abroad.\n\nNevertheless, the benefits are not evenly shared. Member states differ greatly in income and development, and small firms often find it difficult to compete with large multinational corporations. Workers may also face competition from migrants who are willing to accept lower pay. Governments therefore need to invest in training and to support small businesses so that <b>they</b> can take advantage of the new opportunities."
