        "answer": 3,
        "passage": "Southeast Asia attracts tens of millions of tourists every year. Visitors come for its beaches, temples, historic towns and food, and many travel from one country to another on a single trip. ASEAN has encouraged this movement by simplifying visa rules and by promoting joint tourism campaigns that present the region as a single destination.\n\nThe effects on local economies are considerable. Hotels, restaurants, guides and craft sellers all depend on visitors, and tourism supports millions of jobs. In small communities, tourism income may be the main <b>alternative</b> to farming or fishing. <u>However, an economy that relies too heavily on tourism is vulnerable to sudden shocks, as the pandemic showed when borders closed and visitor numbers fell sharply.</u>\n\nTourism also promotes cultural exchange. Travellers encounter <b>unfamiliar</b> customs, languages and religions, and many return home with greater respect for people who live differently from them. Regional events, such as the Southeast Asian Games, which are held every two years, bring athletes and supporters from all member states together. Such contact helps to build the sense of a shared community among people who might otherwise never meet.\n\nAt the same time, mass tourism can damage the places that it depends on. Crowds may wear out fragile temples and beaches, local residents may be priced out of their own neighbourhoods, and traditions may be changed to suit visitors' tastes. Many destinations are therefore experimenting with limits on visitor numbers and with tourism that gives local people a greater say. Visitors who respect local customs and behave responsibly generally leave a more positive impression than <b>those</b> who ignore them."
