        "passage": "At a week-long ASEAN youth camp in Da Nang, thirty students from eight Southeast Asian countries arrived feeling nervous about their different accents and customs. At first, the challenge of dealing with ASEAN youth volunteering seemed <b>unfamiliar</b> to everyone involved, as early expectations did not match everyday reality.\n\n<u>Once they were divided into mixed national teams to build a solar-powered water filter for a coastal village, shyness quickly disappeared.</u> This practical adjustment allowed the Vietnamese and ASEAN student volunteers to see how <b>they</b> could make steady progress without unnecessary conflict.\n\nBy the end of the week, the once hesitant strangers felt united as close friends who stayed in touch online long after returning home. What observers found most <b>memorable</b> was how calmly participants handled unexpected setbacks once open communication and practical routines had been established.\n\nParticipants concluded that solving a real community problem together is the surest bridge between cultures in Southeast Asia."
