        "passage": "Unlike competitive debating tournaments where one side wins by defeating the other, a Model ASEAN Summit follows the decision-making style of ASEAN. Researchers and educators studying the ASEAN way of consensus-building note that this trend has become increasingly <b>prominent</b> in recent years, as student delegates representing countries from Brunei to Viet Nam must negotiate until they reach consensus on shared resolutions.\n\nCloser observation of everyday situations reveals why the ASEAN way of consensus-building matters so much in practice. <u>Stepping into the shoes of another Southeast Asian nation teaches students to view regional problems from multiple perspectives rather than just their own.</u> This pattern shows that practical engagement with the ASEAN way of consensus-building often reshapes long-held assumptions far more effectively than passive instruction.\n\n[I] When schools and communities introduce structured initiatives focused on the ASEAN way of consensus-building, participants often respond with unexpected enthusiasm. [II] Many report that working through realistic examples together helps clarify points that previously caused confusion or disagreement. [III] Coordinators observe that this collaborative atmosphere encourages even hesitant participants to contribute their own perspectives. [IV] Over time, these shared experiences build habits of cooperation that extend well beyond the original programme.\n\nSome sceptics occasionally question whether short community programmes on the ASEAN way of consensus-building can produce <b>beneficial</b> results without major structural reforms. Organisers generally reply that while large-scale policy matters, equipping everyday people with practical skills and mutual understanding remains the clearest foundation for sustainable progress."
