        "passage": "From Ha Long Bay and Hoi An in Viet Nam to Angkor in Cambodia and Mount Kinabalu in Malaysia, Southeast Asia is home to dozens of renowned heritage sites. Researchers and educators studying sustainable tourism across ASEAN heritage sites note that this trend has become increasingly <b>recognised</b> in recent years, as ASEAN member states cooperate through the ASEAN Heritage Parks network and sustainable tourism guidelines to protect these treasures.\n\nCloser observation of everyday situations reveals why sustainable tourism across ASEAN heritage sites matters so much in practice. <u>When visitors choose community-guided eco-tours and carry back all their litter, tourism becomes a source of funding for conservation rather than a threat.</u> This pattern shows that practical engagement with sustainable tourism across ASEAN heritage sites often reshapes long-held assumptions far more effectively than passive instruction.\n\n[I] When schools and communities introduce structured initiatives focused on sustainable tourism across ASEAN heritage sites, participants often respond with unexpected enthusiasm. [II] Many report that working through realistic examples together helps clarify points that previously caused confusion or disagreement. [III] Coordinators observe that this collaborative atmosphere encourages even hesitant participants to contribute their own perspectives. [IV] Over time, these shared experiences build habits of cooperation that extend well beyond the original programme.\n\nSome sceptics occasionally question whether short community programmes on sustainable tourism across ASEAN heritage sites can produce <b>meaningful</b> results without major structural reforms. Organisers generally reply that while large-scale policy matters, equipping everyday people with practical skills and mutual understanding remains the clearest foundation for sustainable progress."
