        "answer": 0,
        "passage": "The Mekong Delta, in the south of Viet Nam, is often called the country's rice bowl. Its <b>fertile</b> land produces a large share of the rice, fruit and seafood that the country consumes and exports. Yet the delta is also one of the regions most exposed to the effects of global warming, because most of the land lies only a little above sea level.\n\nOne serious problem is salt water intrusion. When sea levels rise and river flows decrease during the dry season, salt water moves further inland and enters canals and fields. Farmers whose crops are damaged by salt often lose a large part of their income. <u>Faced with such losses, many families have changed what they grow, replacing rice with fruit trees or with shrimp farming that tolerates salty water.</u> Others have decided to leave the countryside in search of work in the cities.\n\nAdapting to the new conditions is not easy, but progress is being made. Scientists have developed rice varieties that are more <b>resistant</b> to salt, and farmers are being trained to use water more carefully. In some provinces, local authorities have built sluice gates and reservoirs that store fresh water for the dry season. Such measures have helped many communities to protect their harvests and livelihoods.\n\nExperts stress, however, that local efforts alone cannot solve the problem. The delta also suffers from sand mining, the building of dams upstream and the pumping of too much groundwater, all of which cause the land to sink. Unless <b>these</b> are controlled, even the best adaptation measures may fail. Protecting the delta therefore depends on cooperation among provinces, countries and communities."
