        "passage": "Hoi An, in central Viet Nam, was an important trading port from the sixteenth to the eighteenth century, where merchants from Japan, China and Europe met. Their influence can still be seen in the town's narrow streets, wooden houses and shrines. In 1999, UNESCO recognised Hoi An Ancient Town as a World Heritage Site, noting that it is an <b>exceptionally</b> well-preserved example of a traditional trading port in Southeast Asia.\n\nPreservation has not been easy. The town lies close to a river and suffers regular floods, which damage the old wooden buildings. <u>Heavy rain and humidity also cause the timber to rot, so the houses require constant repair using traditional methods and materials.</u> Local authorities have also limited traffic in the old quarter to reduce vibration and pollution.\n\n[I] Tourism, meanwhile, has brought both benefits and problems. [II] Income from entrance tickets supports restoration work, and many residents earn a living by running guesthouses, restaurants and craft shops. [III] On the other hand, large crowds can make the streets noisy, and rising prices have forced some families to move out. [IV] Some residents worry that the town may become a stage for tourists rather than a place to live.\n\nTo keep the town alive, officials encourage residents to continue their traditional occupations, such as lantern making, tailoring and cooking local dishes. Visitors are asked to respect the residents' privacy and to buy locally made products. In this way, <b>they</b> help to ensure that heritage protection also supports the community that lives with it."
