        "answer": 3,
        "passage": "Intangible cultural heritage—such as folk songs, traditional instruments and ritual dances—survives only when each generation learns and performs it. Researchers and educators studying preserving traditional folk music note that this trend has become increasingly <b>significant</b> in recent years, as UNESCO-recognised traditions like quan ho, ca tru and don ca tai tu rely on young people discovering their beauty.\n\nCloser observation of everyday situations reveals why preserving traditional folk music matters so much in practice. <u>Hearing a master musician play the single-stringed dan bau live in a quiet room often moves teenagers far more deeply than a textbook description ever could.</u> This pattern shows that practical engagement with preserving traditional folk music often reshapes long-held assumptions far more effectively than passive instruction.\n\n[I] When schools and communities introduce structured initiatives focused on preserving traditional folk music, participants often respond with unexpected enthusiasm. [II] Many report that working through realistic examples together helps clarify points that previously caused confusion or disagreement. [III] Coordinators observe that this collaborative atmosphere encourages even hesitant participants to contribute their own perspectives. [IV] Over time, these shared experiences build habits of cooperation that extend well beyond the original programme.\n\nSome sceptics occasionally question whether short community programmes on preserving traditional folk music can produce <b>substantial</b> results without major structural reforms. Organisers generally reply that while large-scale policy matters, equipping everyday people with practical skills and mutual understanding remains the clearest foundation for sustainable progress."
