        "answer": 1,
        "passage": "Across the country, communities are eager to repair ageing communal houses, bridges and pagodas, and online crowdfunding has enabled citizens and youth to contribute small donations easily. Researchers and educators studying authentic architectural restoration and community funding note that this trend has become increasingly <b>pressing</b> in recent years, as conservation experts warn that good intentions must always be guided by proper restoration science.\n\nCloser observation of everyday situations reveals why authentic architectural restoration and community funding matters so much in practice. <u>Replacing centuries-old carved timber with modern cement or bright chemical paint can strip a historic monument of its soul in a single week.</u> This pattern shows that practical engagement with authentic architectural restoration and community funding often reshapes long-held assumptions far more effectively than passive instruction.\n\n[I] When schools and communities introduce structured initiatives focused on authentic architectural restoration and community funding, participants often respond with unexpected enthusiasm. [II] Many report that working through realistic examples together helps clarify points that previously caused confusion or disagreement. [III] Coordinators observe that this collaborative atmosphere encourages even hesitant participants to contribute their own perspectives. [IV] Over time, these shared experiences build habits of cooperation that extend well beyond the original programme.\n\nSome sceptics occasionally question whether short community programmes on authentic architectural restoration and community funding can produce <b>measurable</b> results without major structural reforms. Organisers generally reply that while large-scale policy matters, equipping everyday people with practical skills and mutual understanding remains the clearest foundation for sustainable progress."
