        "answer": 3,
        "passage": "Each year, hundreds of thousands of Vietnamese students sit the national high school graduation exam. For many of them, the next question is what to do after the exam. The traditional answer was to go to university, because a degree was seen as the safest route to a good job. Today, however, more <b>alternatives</b> are available, and many young people are considering them seriously.\n\nUniversities offer academic study in fields such as science, law and the humanities, and a degree can open doors to professional careers. Vocational colleges, by contrast, focus on practical skills for specific jobs, and their courses are usually shorter and cheaper. <u>Neither route is better in every case: the right choice depends on a student's abilities, interests and the career he or she wishes to follow.</u> Some students even combine them, starting with a vocational course and moving on to university later.\n\n[I] Whatever path they choose, students are advised to gather information early. [II] School counsellors, open days and career fairs can all help them to understand what each option involves. [III] Talking to people who already work in a field is equally useful, because they can describe its daily realities rather than its image. [IV] Students should also consider the costs of study and look for scholarships or part-time work to help with expenses.\n\nParents also play a role, but experts warn them against making the decision for their children. Students who choose for themselves usually stay more motivated than <b>those</b> who are pushed into a course they dislike. Parents can be most helpful by listening, sharing their experience and supporting the choice that their children make after careful thought."
