        "answer": 1,
        "passage": "Online learning has changed the options available to school-leavers. Through websites and apps, students can study foreign languages, computer programming or business skills at their own pace, often at little or no cost. Many universities now offer entire degrees online, while others combine online lessons with classroom teaching.\n\nThe advantages are obvious. Students who live far from major cities can attend lectures given by experts who would otherwise be out of reach, and working students can fit lessons around their jobs. Learners who set a fixed time for study each day are far more likely to finish a course than <b>those</b> who study only when they feel like it. <u>Online study therefore rewards regular habits more than natural talent.</u>\n\nNevertheless, online learning demands self-control. Without a teacher in the room, it is easy to <b>postpone</b> lessons or give up when a topic becomes difficult. Students may also miss the friendships and discussions that campus life provides. Some courses offer little personal support, so learners should check whether tutors answer questions promptly before they pay for them.\n\nFor these reasons, many experts suggest a balanced approach. Students might begin with a free course to test their interest, check that a certificate is recognised by employers or universities, and combine online study with practical experience. Used wisely, online learning can be a valuable <b>supplement</b> to traditional education rather than a replacement for it."
