        "passage": "For students who enjoy deep theoretical study, laboratory research or professions such as medicine, law, teaching and engineering, university remains the primary pathway after high school. Researchers and educators studying informed university major selection note that this trend has become increasingly <b>visible</b> in recent years, as many school-leavers pick a university major simply because friends are applying for it or because the title sounds fashionable.\n\nCloser observation of everyday situations reveals why informed university major selection matters so much in practice. <u>Reading the year-by-year subject list of a university degree prevents unpleasant surprises about how much mathematics, writing or lab work is required.</u> This pattern shows that practical engagement with informed university major selection often reshapes long-held assumptions far more effectively than passive instruction.\n\n[I] When schools and communities introduce structured initiatives focused on informed university major selection, participants often respond with unexpected enthusiasm. [II] Many report that working through realistic examples together helps clarify points that previously caused confusion or disagreement. [III] Coordinators observe that this collaborative atmosphere encourages even hesitant participants to contribute their own perspectives. [IV] Over time, these shared experiences build habits of cooperation that extend well beyond the original programme.\n\nSome sceptics occasionally question whether short community programmes on informed university major selection can produce <b>practical</b> results without major structural reforms. Organisers generally reply that while large-scale policy matters, equipping everyday people with practical skills and mutual understanding remains the clearest foundation for sustainable progress."
