        "passage": "Learning to manage money is one of the most important steps towards independence. Many young people leave home for university or work with little experience of budgeting, and they are surprised by how quickly money disappears. Rent, food, transport and phone bills are fixed costs that must be paid first, while entertainment and shopping are <b>optional</b>.\n\nThe first step is to keep a record of income and spending. Even a simple notebook or phone app can show where money goes each month. <u>Once young people see how much they spend on small items such as drinks and snacks, they often discover that these costs add up to a surprising amount.</u> They can then set limits and decide which expenses are really necessary.\n\n[I] Saving is the second habit to build. [II] Experts suggest putting aside a fixed amount as soon as money is received, instead of saving whatever remains at the end of the month. [III] An emergency fund is particularly useful, since unexpected costs such as medical bills or repairs can otherwise force young people to borrow. [IV] Over time, even small regular savings can grow into a useful safety net.\n\nFinally, young people should be careful about borrowing. Credit cards and online loans may seem convenient, but high interest rates can turn small debts into serious problems. People who learn to live within their means usually feel less anxious about money than <b>those</b> who rely on borrowing. Parents and schools can help by discussing money openly and by giving teenagers small amounts to manage on their own."
