        "passage": "For many students, starting university means living away from home for the first time. They enjoy new freedom, but they also face unfamiliar tasks: shopping, cooking, washing clothes and paying bills. It is therefore not surprising that many feel overwhelmed during the first weeks.\n\nHomesickness is common, and it affects students who are far from their families more strongly than <b>those</b> who can visit them regularly. Missing familiar food, friends and routines is natural. <u>The feeling usually becomes weaker as students build new friendships and establish routines of their own.</u> Staying in touch with relatives through video calls can help, but experts advise students not to spend all their free time in front of a screen.\n\nUniversities can support newcomers in several ways. Orientation weeks introduce students to the campus and to each other, and counselling services offer advice to anyone who feels <b>anxious</b>. Many universities also run workshops on cooking, budgeting and time management, which help students to develop practical skills.\n\nStudents can do much for themselves, too. Joining clubs and societies is an effective way to meet people with similar interests, and a regular timetable for study, meals and sleep gives each day a clear <b>structure</b>. Above all, they should remember that finding it difficult at first does not mean that they have made the wrong choice; it simply means they are learning to stand on their own feet."
