        "passage": "Growing independent does not mean acting on impulse; rather, it means learning how to make well-considered decisions and stand accountable for their results. Researchers and educators studying responsible decision-making note that this trend has become increasingly <b>prominent</b> in recent years, as teenagers face daily choices—from how to balance club duties with exams to how to respond when friends suggest risky behaviour.\n\nCloser observation of everyday situations reveals why responsible decision-making matters so much in practice. <u>Mature decision-makers willingly consult parents or teachers for perspective, yet take personal ownership of the final choice.</u> This pattern shows that practical engagement with responsible decision-making often reshapes long-held assumptions far more effectively than passive instruction.\n\n[I] When schools and communities introduce structured initiatives focused on responsible decision-making, participants often respond with unexpected enthusiasm. [II] Many report that working through realistic examples together helps clarify points that previously caused confusion or disagreement. [III] Coordinators observe that this collaborative atmosphere encourages even hesitant participants to contribute their own perspectives. [IV] Over time, these shared experiences build habits of cooperation that extend well beyond the original programme.\n\nSome sceptics occasionally question whether short community programmes on responsible decision-making can produce <b>beneficial</b> results without major structural reforms. Organisers generally reply that while large-scale policy matters, equipping everyday people with practical skills and mutual understanding remains the clearest foundation for sustainable progress."
