        "passage": "Cyberbullying means using the internet or mobile phones to hurt, threaten or embarrass another person. It may involve nasty messages, humiliating photos or rumours spread through social media. Because messages can be sent at any hour, victims often feel that there is no <b>escape</b>, even in their own homes.\n\nThe effects can be serious. Victims may become anxious, lose interest in school and avoid their friends. Some find it hard to sleep or concentrate. <u>Because offensive posts can be copied and shared instantly, the harm may spread much further than the person who wrote them ever intended.</u> Many victims say that the fear of being judged by a large online audience is the hardest part.\n\n[I] Bullies often feel safe because they can hide behind screens. [II] They may not see the reactions of their victims, so they fail to realise how much pain they cause. [III] Others join in simply because their friends do, without thinking about the consequences. [IV] Schools therefore need to teach students that online behaviour has real effects on real people.\n\nWhat can be done? Victims should keep evidence, such as screenshots, and report the behaviour to a teacher, parent or the platform itself. Bystanders can help by refusing to share hurtful content and by supporting the victim. Many schools now have clear rules and counselling services, and students who are trained as peer supporters are often more trusted by victims than <b>those</b> who are simply told to behave."
