        "passage": "In many large cities, thousands of people sleep on the streets or in temporary shelters. Some have lost their jobs, others have been forced out of their homes by high rents, and some are fleeing family conflict. Homelessness is often seen as a personal failure, but in reality, it usually results from a combination of causes that are beyond an individual's control.\n\nLiving without a stable home affects every part of life. It is hard to keep a job without a place to wash and rest, and children who move frequently find it difficult to keep up with schoolwork. <u>Without an address, people may also be unable to obtain documents, healthcare or benefits that they are entitled to.</u> Homeless people are therefore more likely to fall ill than <b>those</b> who have secure housing.\n\nSeveral approaches have proved effective. Some cities provide emergency shelters, while others follow a \"housing first\" policy, which gives people a permanent home before asking them to solve other problems such as unemployment. Charities offer meals, clothing and job training, and volunteers listen to people's stories. Such support helps homeless people to regain their <b>dignity</b>.\n\nOrdinary people can contribute in simple ways. Donating food or warm clothes, volunteering at a shelter or supporting organisations that build affordable housing all make a difference. Equally important is to treat homeless people with respect, rather than to <b>ignore</b> them or judge them."
