        "answer": 2,
        "passage": "During adolescence, the desire to belong to a group of friends is strong and natural, yet peer influence can work in two very different directions. Researchers and educators studying resisting negative peer pressure note that this trend has become increasingly <b>prevalent</b> in recent years, as while positive peer groups encourage study and sport, negative peer pressure pushes teenagers to skip classes, mock others or try harmful habits.\n\nCloser observation of everyday situations reveals why resisting negative peer pressure matters so much in practice. <u>Practising a short, calm refusal—or suggesting a safe alternative activity—allows a teenager to say no without starting an argument.</u> This pattern shows that practical engagement with resisting negative peer pressure often reshapes long-held assumptions far more effectively than passive instruction.\n\n[I] When schools and communities introduce structured initiatives focused on resisting negative peer pressure, participants often respond with unexpected enthusiasm. [II] Many report that working through realistic examples together helps clarify points that previously caused confusion or disagreement. [III] Coordinators observe that this collaborative atmosphere encourages even hesitant participants to contribute their own perspectives. [IV] Over time, these shared experiences build habits of cooperation that extend well beyond the original programme.\n\nSome sceptics occasionally question whether short community programmes on resisting negative peer pressure can produce <b>consistent</b> results without major structural reforms. Organisers generally reply that while large-scale policy matters, equipping everyday people with practical skills and mutual understanding remains the clearest foundation for sustainable progress."
