        "passage": "While educational access has expanded greatly, children from low-income or flood-affected rural families still face real obstacles to finishing secondary school. Researchers and educators studying supporting disadvantaged students to stay in school note that this trend has become increasingly <b>recognised</b> in recent years, as long walks on mountain roads, lack of reference books or family financial hardship can threaten a student's schooling.\n\nCloser observation of everyday situations reveals why supporting disadvantaged students to stay in school matters so much in practice. <u>Providing a refurbished bicycle, a set of textbooks and a modest meal scholarship removes the immediate barriers that force disadvantaged teenagers to leave school early.</u> This pattern shows that practical engagement with supporting disadvantaged students to stay in school often reshapes long-held assumptions far more effectively than passive instruction.\n\n[I] When schools and communities introduce structured initiatives focused on supporting disadvantaged students to stay in school, participants often respond with unexpected enthusiasm. [II] Many report that working through realistic examples together helps clarify points that previously caused confusion or disagreement. [III] Coordinators observe that this collaborative atmosphere encourages even hesitant participants to contribute their own perspectives. [IV] Over time, these shared experiences build habits of cooperation that extend well beyond the original programme.\n\nSome sceptics occasionally question whether short community programmes on supporting disadvantaged students to stay in school can produce <b>meaningful</b> results without major structural reforms. Organisers generally reply that while large-scale policy matters, equipping everyday people with practical skills and mutual understanding remains the clearest foundation for sustainable progress."
