        "answer": 1,
        "passage": "An inclusive society is measured by how naturally people of all physical and sensory abilities can study, work, travel and take part in community life. Researchers and educators studying disability inclusion and accessible schools note that this trend has become increasingly <b>prominent</b> in recent years, as steep steps without ramps, unlabelled audio resources or awkward social exclusion still create unnecessary obstacles for many young people with disabilities.\n\nCloser observation of everyday situations reveals why disability inclusion and accessible schools matters so much in practice. <u>What students with disabilities ask for is not pity, but practical accessibility—such as clear ramps, screen-reader textbooks and being invited into group projects as equal partners.</u> This pattern shows that practical engagement with disability inclusion and accessible schools often reshapes long-held assumptions far more effectively than passive instruction.\n\n[I] When schools and communities introduce structured initiatives focused on disability inclusion and accessible schools, participants often respond with unexpected enthusiasm. [II] Many report that working through realistic examples together helps clarify points that previously caused confusion or disagreement. [III] Coordinators observe that this collaborative atmosphere encourages even hesitant participants to contribute their own perspectives. [IV] Over time, these shared experiences build habits of cooperation that extend well beyond the original programme.\n\nSome sceptics occasionally question whether short community programmes on disability inclusion and accessible schools can produce <b>beneficial</b> results without major structural reforms. Organisers generally reply that while large-scale policy matters, equipping everyday people with practical skills and mutual understanding remains the clearest foundation for sustainable progress."
