        "passage": "Mangrove forests grow along tropical coasts, where fresh water from rivers mixes with salt water from the sea. Their roots rise above the muddy ground and form a dense network that is difficult for people to cross. This may look <b>inhospitable</b>, but the roots provide shelter for a remarkable variety of fish, crabs and shrimps.\n\nThe ecosystem also provides protection for people. <u>Mangrove roots slow down waves and strong winds, so the forest acts as a natural barrier that reduces damage to villages during storms.</u> The roots also trap mud and sand, which helps to protect the coastline from being worn away. In addition, local people collect shrimps, crabs and honey from the forest without cutting it down.\n\n[I] Despite their value, mangroves have been disappearing in many countries. [II] Large areas have been cleared to make shrimp farms, ports and housing. [III] In Viet Nam, some mangrove forests were also damaged during the war and had to be replanted. [IV] Today, the Can Gio forest near Ho Chi Minh City is a successful example of restoration and is recognised as a biosphere reserve.\n\nExperts argue that protecting mangroves is cheaper than repairing the damage caused by their loss. Governments can limit the conversion of forests into farms, and communities can be paid for looking after them. Visitors can also help by following paths, taking their rubbish home and buying products from local people. When residents benefit from the forest, <b>they</b> have a strong reason to protect it."
