        "answer": 0,
        "passage": "An invasive species is an animal, plant or other organism that is introduced to a new area and spreads quickly, harming the native species. Because it has left behind the predators and diseases that kept its numbers under control at home, it can multiply rapidly in its new environment.\n\nThe consequences can be severe. Invasive plants may cover rivers and lakes, blocking sunlight and reducing oxygen in the water, so that native fish cannot survive. Introduced animals may eat native species or compete with them for food. <u>Because native species have not developed ways of defending themselves against the newcomers, they can disappear within a few years.</u> Ecosystems that have lost their natural balance are also more <b>vulnerable</b> to further damage.\n\nInvasive species often arrive by accident. Seeds may travel in cargo, small animals may hide in ships, and aquarium fish or pets that are released into the wild can establish new populations. Some species, however, are introduced deliberately, for example to control pests, and then turn out to cause <b>unforeseen</b> problems.\n\nPrevention is the most effective strategy. Countries that inspect cargo carefully usually suffer fewer invasions than <b>those</b> that do not, and travellers can help by not carrying plants, seeds or animals across borders. Once a species is established, removing it can be slow and expensive, so early detection is vital. Public awareness campaigns encourage people to report unusual species and to avoid releasing unwanted pets."
