        "passage": "When roads, industrial zones and housing estates divide a landscape into small, separated patches, ecologists call the problem 'habitat fragmentation'. Researchers and educators studying connecting fragmented habitats with green corridors note that this trend has become increasingly <b>noticeable</b> in recent years, as small isolated patches of trees act like islands where birds, amphibians and insects struggle to find enough food or mates across the seasons.\n\nCloser observation of everyday situations reveals why connecting fragmented habitats with green corridors matters so much in practice. <u>Planting continuous green corridors—along riverbanks, railway verges and wide avenues—turns isolated green islands back into a connected ecological network.</u> This pattern shows that practical engagement with connecting fragmented habitats with green corridors often reshapes long-held assumptions far more effectively than passive instruction.\n\n[I] When schools and communities introduce structured initiatives focused on connecting fragmented habitats with green corridors, participants often respond with unexpected enthusiasm. [II] Many report that working through realistic examples together helps clarify points that previously caused confusion or disagreement. [III] Coordinators observe that this collaborative atmosphere encourages even hesitant participants to contribute their own perspectives. [IV] Over time, these shared experiences build habits of cooperation that extend well beyond the original programme.\n\nSome sceptics occasionally question whether short community programmes on connecting fragmented habitats with green corridors can produce <b>durable</b> results without major structural reforms. Organisers generally reply that while large-scale policy matters, equipping everyday people with practical skills and mutual understanding remains the clearest foundation for sustainable progress."
